1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,390
JAMIE: Nasdaq is down zero point eight-two percent as Iran tensions spike, but T S M C just posted a sixty-eight percent revenue surge—which means the chip market's basically running on fumes and miracles right now.

2
00:00:11,390 --> 00:00:34,487
MAYA: Meta's down one point seven-one percent, but here's where it gets funny—the actual Meta glasses, the ones they're banking on as the AI hardware layer, just got torched by fashion editors and product reviewers. Turns out when you design glasses that make the wearer look like they're perpetually squinting at a distant fire alarm, the investment thesis doesn't survive contact with a mirror. The market loves the narrative; nobody loves wearing it.

3
00:00:34,487 --> 00:00:58,534
ALEX: Markets took a step back today. S&P down zero point three-one percent, Nasdaq down zero point eight-two percent—that's the second-worst performer on the board. The V I X jumped eight point six-five percent, which tells you people are pricing in actual risk rather than pretending geopolitical news is just noise. Energy was the only thing that made sense: Exxon Mobil up two point seven-eight percent. When Iran tensions spike, people stop theorizing and start hedging.

4
00:00:58,534 --> 00:01:05,178
JAMIE: So the chip story from earlier—T S M C's revenue surge—that's not translating to the rest of the sector today?

5
00:01:05,178 --> 00:01:28,592
ALEX: No. A M D down two point one-five percent, Oracle down three point zero-three percent. Oracle's the worst loser on the board. What you're seeing is a flight from big software and semiconductors into things that actually benefit from geopolitical friction. Enterprise cloud software gets repriced when the world's on edge. Oracle's been riding the AI narrative hard—now it's just another expensive software stock when people are worried about supply chains and sanctions.

6
00:01:28,592 --> 00:01:31,439
MAYA: But Salesforce is up three point five-two percent?

7
00:01:31,439 --> 00:01:51,056
ALEX: Salesforce got a bid because it's not Oracle. Relative strength in a sector that's getting hammered is still strength. Tesla down two point two-two percent, Meta down one point seven-one percent—the narrative stocks are all down. Salesforce just happens to be the least-hated software name today. That's not a vote of confidence. That's a vote of 'we'll sell this one last.'

8
00:01:51,056 --> 00:02:08,458
ALEX: The sixty-eight percent number is real, but here's what matters: that's year-over-year, and it's coming off a depressed base. T S M C's June revenue was strong. That's the highest monthly number they've reported in two years, which means they're actually recovering from a previous hangover, not necessarily sprinting into some new AI-driven future.

9
00:02:08,458 --> 00:02:14,786
JAMIE: Wait—so the headline sounds like 'chips are on fire' but what you're really saying is 'chips stopped being dead'?

10
00:02:14,786 --> 00:02:34,087
ALEX: Exactly. The semiconductor industry got flattened when the market realized that everyone had ordered way too much inventory. T S M C's revenue recovered. That's growth, but it's recovery from a crater. The question everyone should be asking is whether that crater is actually filled or whether we're just seeing AI companies finally deploying the chips they ordered months ago.

11
00:02:34,087 --> 00:02:36,934
MAYA: But the AI companies are still spending, right?

12
00:02:36,934 --> 00:02:55,286
ALEX: But the real test is whether the people ordering from T S M C are still ordering at the same pace in Q3 and Q4, or whether June was the last gasp before everyone realizes they've built enough capacity. T S M C's not going to tell us that until earnings. All we have is one month.

13
00:02:55,286 --> 00:03:00,348
ALEX: Geopolitical risk isn't a talking point anymore; it's repricing semiconductor supply chains in real time.

14
00:03:00,348 --> 00:03:06,360
JAMIE: So when Iran tensions spike, memory chip makers get hit because they can't manufacture through a supply blockade.

15
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:14,586
ALEX: Exactly. Supply chains run through vulnerable corridors. One escalation and the cost of moving materials just went up by a factor nobody's modeled into guidance yet.

16
00:03:14,586 --> 00:03:25,027
MAYA: But Wall Street's about to make a lot of money off this chaos anyway—big banks are poised to report record revenue because volatility just handed them the quarter they were dreaming about.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/13/bank-earnings-jpmorgan-chase-goldman-sachs-bank-of-america.html

17
00:03:25,027 --> 00:03:29,140
JAMIE: So the financial system's best quarter is literally built on regional conflict.

18
00:03:29,140 --> 00:03:38,316
ALEX: Add in the volatility spike, and you've got commercial lending rebounding at the same time. That's the trifecta. But it's not sustainable—it's just the calendar aligning with chaos.

19
00:03:38,316 --> 00:03:49,706
MAYA: Europe just moved to hard age limits on teen social media access—not recommendations, not guidelines, actual restrictions. The regulatory playbook is now coming for major platforms, and the difference is it's actually sticking this time.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/tech/964565/eu-children-social-media-online-safety-rules

20
00:03:49,706 --> 00:03:57,300
JAMIE: So Meta's rally on AI cloud dreams doesn't account for the fact that their user base in Europe is about to get legally smaller.

21
00:03:57,300 --> 00:04:07,425
ALEX: Paramount and Warner Bros. Discovery are about to get sued by multiple state attorneys general before the merger even closes—consolidation in media is hitting a wall that scale can't break through.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/13/paramount-wbd-merger-lawsuit.html

22
00:04:07,425 --> 00:04:20,714
MAYA: Lorde just told a crowd that Ray-Ban Meta AI glasses are 'not sexy,' and everyone in that venue knew exactly what she meant. A pop star just became the product reviewer that Meta's entire engineering team was hoping would never exist.

23
00:04:20,714 --> 00:04:38,432
JAMIE: So Meta's got this whole narrative about AI glasses as the next computing layer, the future of how we interact with the world, and Lorde walks on stage and says the thing nobody at Meta wants to hear: they look bad. That's not a technical critique. That's a cultural death sentence for a hardware play.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/964539/lorde-says-ray-ban-meta-ai-glasses-are-not-sexy

24
00:04:38,432 --> 00:04:53,936
ALEX: Meta shipped a limited volume of Ray-Ban units. When the gap between your installed base and a competitor's annual volume is that wide, and a musician on a festival stage is your most effective marketing feedback, you've got a design problem that no amount of AI narrative fixes.

25
00:04:53,936 --> 00:04:58,049
JAMIE: On the tape tomorrow—we've got some earnings that actually matter in the noise.

26
00:04:58,049 --> 00:05:16,717
ALEX: Plug Power reports on July thirteenth. They've been the hydrogen fuel cell play that everyone cycled through multiple times already—first as the climate narrative, then as the infrastructure spend, now as the 'maybe this actually works' story. Their last quarter was supposed to show whether the capex is finally translating to real revenue or just burning through cash.

27
00:05:16,717 --> 00:05:31,904
MAYA: Also on the thirteenth—Alto Neuroscience. They're a biotech that got FDA approval for a depression treatment. The stock's been dead money since the IPO, but if they can show the label's expanding or the patient pipeline is real, this could be the day someone remembers they exist.

28
00:05:31,904 --> 00:05:39,181
MAYA: T S M C's sixty-eight percent is what happens when you stop digging and find out you're only halfway to the bottom.

29
00:05:39,181 --> 00:05:45,193
ALEX: Which means the real question isn't whether chips recovered—it's whether the people buying them remember what they ordered.

30
00:05:45,193 --> 00:05:49,622
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
